<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  POV one-pager template.

  This file is the answer to claim 3: two POVs for two customers have to look
  like the same product, and "both satisfy rule 24" does not get you there.
  Two documents can each honour "whitespace is functional" and share no visual
  DNA at all. The type scale, the colour, the section order and the diagram
  treatment live HERE so they are the same every time, rather than being
  reconstructed from the model's taste on the day.

  Replace every {{PLACEHOLDER}}. Do not restyle. If a customer needs a
  different look, copy this file, change it, and point POV_TEMPLATE_PATH at
  the copy — that is what the override is for, and it is better than editing
  the shipped one because the shipped one is what makes two POVs match.

  Rule references in the comments are to specs/POV-SPEC.md. They mark the
  elements whose POSITION is load-bearing; moving them breaks the rule, not
  just the look.

  No date stamp anywhere, by rule 25.

  The page geometry is part of the template, not a dial to turn per customer.
  Rule 16 is one page, and the tiles, the path-forward list and the signature
  arrived (#8) on a layout whose margins were set before they existed — so the
  margins were reset once, here, as part of adding them. A run that bleeds
  tightens its COPY; it does not reopen this number. `test/one-page.test.ts`
  renders the worked example and fails at two pages, which is how the regression
  that prompted this comment was found after eight green suites missed it.
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{CUSTOMER_NAME}} — A Perspective</title>
<style>
  @page { size: letter; margin: 0.42in; }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    font-family: 'Red Hat Display', 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;
    color: #151515; margin: 0; padding: 0.33in 0.55in;
    font-size: 10.5pt; line-height: 1.40; max-width: 8.5in;
  }
  h1 { font-size: 19pt; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 2pt; letter-spacing: -0.4pt; }
  .sub { color: #6a6e73; font-size: 10pt; margin: 0 0 12pt; }
  h2 {
    font-size: 8.5pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1.1pt;
    color: #6a6e73; margin: 8pt 0 3pt; font-weight: 700;
  }
  p { margin: 0 0 5pt; }
  strong { font-weight: 700; }
  .callout {
    border-left: 4px solid #EE0000; background: #FDF0F0;
    padding: 8pt 13pt; margin: 9pt 0;
  }
  .callout p { margin: 0; font-size: 11.5pt; line-height: 1.45; }
  .bridge {
    display: flex; align-items: stretch; gap: 10pt;
    margin: 11pt 0; text-align: center;
  }
  .bridge div {
    flex: 1; border: 1px solid #d2d2d2; border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 8pt 7pt; font-size: 9pt; line-height: 1.32;
  }
  .bridge .mid {
    background: #151515; color: #fff; border-color: #151515;
    font-weight: 700; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    flex: 0 0 24%;
  }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 6pt 0; font-size: 9.5pt; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 3.5pt 8pt 3.5pt 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #F5F5F5; vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: #6a6e73; font-weight: 700; font-size: 8pt; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.6pt; }
  .tradeoff { color: #4d5258; font-style: italic; font-size: 9.5pt; }
  .tiles { display: flex; gap: 14pt; margin: 7pt 0 2pt; }
  .tile { flex: 1; border-top: 3px solid #EE0000; padding: 6pt 0 0; }
  .tile .v { font-size: 17pt; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1; letter-spacing: -0.5pt; }
  .tile .l { font-size: 8.5pt; margin-top: 3pt; line-height: 1.3; }
  .tile .s { font-size: 7.5pt; color: #6a6e73; margin-top: 2pt; }
  ol.path { margin: 2pt 0 0; padding-left: 14pt; font-size: 9.5pt; }
  ol.path li { margin: 0 0 3pt; line-height: 1.38; }
  ol.path strong { display: block; }
  .cta { margin-top: 10pt; padding-top: 8pt; border-top: 2px solid #151515;
         display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between; gap: 18pt; }
  .cta strong { font-size: 11pt; text-align: right; flex: 0 1 auto; }
  .sig { font-size: 8.5pt; color: #6a6e73; line-height: 1.35; text-align: left;
         flex: 0 0 auto; white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

  <!-- Top-left entry point, by rule 21. The headline is the thesis in one
       line, not the customer's name and not a product name. -->
  <h1>{{HEADLINE}}</h1>
  <p class="sub">A perspective for {{CUSTOMER_NAME}}</p>

  <h2>Your Environment</h2>
  <!-- The 2-3 initiatives, named by their INTERNAL program names. Bold each
       one. This paragraph is what proves you did the reading. -->
  <p>{{ENVIRONMENT}}</p>

  <h2>What We're Seeing</h2>
  <!-- Why the initiatives above are currently treated as separate, and the
       cost of that. State the situation; do not yet state the thesis. -->
  <p>{{OBSERVATION}}</p>

  <!-- The convergence callout: the most important element on the page, rule
       32. One idea. If this paragraph could be about another customer, the
       thesis is not specific enough to send. -->
  <div class="callout">
    <p>{{CONVERGENCE_THESIS}}</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Simple visual showing the product bridging both initiatives, rule 33.
       Three boxes: initiative, the thing that connects them, initiative. -->
  <div class="bridge">
    <div><strong>{{BRIDGE_LEFT_TITLE}}</strong><br>{{BRIDGE_LEFT_DETAIL}}</div>
    <div class="mid">{{BRIDGE_MIDDLE}}</div>
    <div><strong>{{BRIDGE_RIGHT_TITLE}}</strong><br>{{BRIDGE_RIGHT_DETAIL}}</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Stat tiles, rule 20. A tile without its attribution is the failure the
       rule names, so the source line is part of the tile and not optional:
       a number large enough to be the first thing read has to say where it
       came from in the same glance. -->
  <div class="tiles">
    <div class="tile">
      <div class="v">{{TILE_1_VALUE}}</div>
      <div class="l">{{TILE_1_LABEL}}</div>
      <div class="s">{{TILE_1_SOURCE}}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tile">
      <div class="v">{{TILE_2_VALUE}}</div>
      <div class="l">{{TILE_2_LABEL}}</div>
      <div class="s">{{TILE_2_SOURCE}}</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Numbers</h2>
  <!-- Peer references. One named company and one concrete metric each, and a
       DIFFERENT reference per persona at the same account. Verify every URL
       resolves before using it. -->
  <table>
    <tr><th>Company</th><th>Situation</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>{{PEER_1_NAME}}</strong></td>
      <td>{{PEER_1_SITUATION}}</td>
      <td>{{PEER_1_OUTCOME}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>{{PEER_2_NAME}}</strong></td>
      <td>{{PEER_2_SITUATION}}</td>
      <td>{{PEER_2_OUTCOME}}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <!-- What the reader does on Monday. Three steps, ordered, each one a thing
       somebody can start — not a phased programme and not a sales stage. The
       validated one-pager spent more words here than on any other section,
       which is the argument for the section existing rather than the thesis
       trailing off into a CTA. -->
  <h2>A Path Forward</h2>
  <ol class="path">
    <li><strong>{{STEP_1_TITLE}}</strong>{{STEP_1_DETAIL}}</li>
    <li><strong>{{STEP_2_TITLE}}</strong>{{STEP_2_DETAIL}}</li>
    <li><strong>{{STEP_3_TITLE}}</strong>{{STEP_3_DETAIL}}</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Exactly one honest limitation, rule 29. Not a disclaimer and not a
       strength in disguise. The reader is deciding whether to trust the rest
       of the page, and this is the paragraph that earns it. -->
  <p class="tradeoff">{{TRADEOFF}}</p>

  <!-- CTA bottom-right, rule 21. A question proposing a short conversation,
       not a request for a meeting to "discuss our solutions". The sender sits
       bottom-LEFT in the same rule: an unsolicited page a stranger receives
       has to say who sent it, and the signature is the only element on the
       page that does. -->
  <div class="cta">
    <div class="sig">{{SENDER_NAME}} &middot; {{SENDER_TITLE}}</div>
    <strong>{{CTA}}</strong>
  </div>

</body>
</html>
